\section{Proved scope and residual open targets in version 1.5}
\label{sec:open-targets}
Version 1.5 proves concrete restricted answers to all six themes.
It does not assert that all six original programs are complete.
Here ``open'' means unresolved by this manuscript, not necessarily
unknown throughout the literature. In particular, a false universal
statement is rejected rather than listed as a conjecture to prove.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.26\textwidth}X@{}}
\toprule Component & Established scope\\\midrule
Multiletter operator theory &
Obstruction, attainment, exact certificates and fixed-slice decision;
conditional quantitative truncation and explicit dyadic precision
are now proved.\\
Positive learning &
Occupation-flow certificates; conditioned spectral--conic bridge;
rank-two interval constructions; frequent-prefix discovery and
estimated-basis learning in the stated bounded-depth promise class.\\
Statistical/numerical guarantees &
Fixed-core frequency events, adaptive selection on a uniform pool
event, exact rational intermediate algebra and exact dyadic output.
No empirical-SVD guarantee is inferred without its own hypotheses.\\
Transfer approximation &
Normalized positive eigenfunction, explicit projective convergence,
and a terminating polynomial approximator with charged bit costs.\\
Executable memory &
Regular-attractor existence law and an optimal table-free executable
for homogeneous separated rational similarities and supplied contractive
global charts; general R6 remains open.\\
Reversible coding &
Integer lifting, correlated-error variance control, and complete
length bounds against specified baseline/model contracts. No general
neural compression or speedup claim.\\\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}

\begin{openproblem}[Sharpness and effective uniformity beyond supplied Stein witnesses]
\label{open:truncation}
Theorem~\ref{thm:quantitative-truncation} settles an explicit
conditional square-root operator bound, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:explicit-dyadic} supplies an a priori precision.
Determine sharp dependence on the witness condition numbers and
contraction deficits, and identify independently verifiable source
classes on which these constants remain useful uniformly under
balanced truncation. Charge obtaining the balancing transform and
certifying the retained tuple. The original spectral-radius-only
argument remains invalid; a universal constant controlled by the
discarded singular value alone is excluded by
Theorem~\ref{thm:separation}.
\end{openproblem}

\begin{openproblem}[Observable class and core discovery beyond the bounded-depth promise]
\label{open:anchors}
Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-pool-learning} now constructs the frequent
prefix pool and its estimated positive basis with a supplied core,
bounded-depth witness promise, and explicit positive margins.
Find broader effectively specified classes and access models that
allow recovery of the core and independently justified structural
certificates, with controlled sample, bit and runtime costs.
For the spectral--conic route, jointly control dictionary size,
prefix masses, conditioning, visits and the full closure/tail budget.
Neither the promise theorem nor small Hankel singular values supplies
these hypotheses for arbitrary sources. Exact finite-prefix closure
is already refuted by Proposition~\ref{prop:golden-prefix-obstruction};
unrestricted bounded-depth certification is refuted by
Proposition~\ref{prop:censored-obstruction}.
\end{openproblem}

\begin{openproblem}[Empirical singular-subspace recovery and stronger prediction metrics]
\label{open:numerical-cone}
Direct-frequency learning with an estimated basis is now proved in
Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-pool-learning}. Theorem~\ref{thm:parametric-rank-two}
also gives a parametric two-state rate on a supplied valid coordinate
interval. What remains for the spectral route is a complete empirical
SVD/Gram perturbation analysis beyond the fixed-block certificate of
Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed-block-svd}, with adaptively
chosen core/dictionary data, positive-cone optimization, and finite
precision in one stated guarantee. For sources outside the supplied
interval class, recover its validity and contraction/denominator
margins from an appropriate access model.
For KL, state tail, smoothing and visit-budget hypotheses explicitly.
Completed-word TV alone does not give uniform prediction after
arbitrarily rare prefixes.
\end{openproblem}

\begin{openproblem}[R6 acceptance and full state-update completeness]
\label{open:r6}
Theorem~\ref{thm:normalized-transfer} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:normalized-polynomial} now supply a normalized
positive target, geometric rate and a terminating polynomial
approximation under effective analytic and contraction hypotheses.
Prove that the full R6 encoder, including coordinate inversion,
all rational acceptance inequalities and inductive update relations,
terminates for the declared separated model class at every precision.
Charge immutable coefficients, scratch, intermediate precision and
every online subroutine. The bound $O_H(p\mathsf M(p))$ for Horner
evaluation is not a bound for the entire state update. Uniform
dependence on a model parameter box needs its own quantitative margins.
\end{openproblem}

\begin{openproblem}[Optimal efficient memory beyond homogeneous address systems]
\label{open:effective-memory}
Theorem~\ref{thm:address-memory} gives
$B_f^*(2^{-p})=sp+O(1)$ with an effective table-free executable for
homogeneous separated similarities and future-observable output.
Theorem~\ref{thm:chart-memory} settles supplied effective globally
contractive charts. Extend these results to unequal contraction ratios,
nonconformal systems without such a chart, and more general initial states, with the
optimal coefficient rather than a worst-contraction upper bound.
Count persistent state, immutable descriptions, output precision,
temporary storage, compilation and update bit costs separately.
The Ahlfors-regular packing law alone does not solve this algorithmic
problem.
\end{openproblem}

\begin{openproblem}[Useful exact-code gains for learned predictive states]
\label{open:exact-codec}
Theorems~\ref{thm:integer-lifting} and \ref{thm:actual-lifting-code}
now prove reversible representation and complete expected-length
bounds for specified models. Identify useful learned-state families
for which all variance, model-mismatch, core and metadata terms can
be bounded or measured and improve a specified admissible baseline.
When a predictor or model is learned, supply matched held-out
evidence with exact reconstruction, complete encode--update--readout
costs, temporary storage and peak allocation. No lossless bijection
can beat every optimal lossless coder; that proposed target is
impossible, not an outstanding empirical goal.
\end{openproblem}

\paragraph{Additional directions.}
Directed metastability or conductance-based KL bounds remain beyond
the rare-word theorem, and overlapping whole-future KL cuts cannot
be added. A multifractal extension must distinguish average and
worst-case error and state its measure; information dimension cannot
simply replace the uniform covering dimension.
